Cambodia-France Hospital Signs Cooperation in Training, Exchange, Provision of Medical Equipment and Referral
(Phnom Penh): Prof. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health of Cambodia, presided over the signing ceremony between the Cambodian and French hospitals on a joint project on training, exchange, provision of medical equipment and referral of patients.
The signing ceremony took place at the Cambodia-China Friendship Hospital Preah Kosomak on April 18, 2023, with the participation of partner hospitals and hospitals of Cambodia, including: Dr. Tan Sokun, Director of the hospital. Cambodia-China Friendship Preah Kosomak, Prof. Luli Kheang, Director of Ang Duong Hospital, Dr. Kak Seila, Director of Battambang Provincial Referral Hospital, Prof. Yin Sina, Director of Kampong Cham Provincial Referral Hospital.
According to Dr. Tan Sokun, the Cambodia-France signing is aimed at four major goals:
1: Human resource training, exchange of study between students and Cambodian doctors to study in France 2: Support of medical equipment to Cambodia 3: Transfer of patients to treatment in France 4: French doctors can come to operate in Cambodia And training students and doctors
"The French partner will visit some of the hospitals that have already signed the cooperation agreement," he said.
Please be informed that the above French hospital is a famous hospital in the country with many specialties, specializing in nose, ear, throat and so on.
(Islamabad): The G7 foreign ministers on Tuesday called for "immediate lifting" of the Taliban government's ban on Afghan women working for NGOs and the United Nations in Afghanistan. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, April 18, 2023.
Under their strict interpretation of Islam, Taliban authorities have imposed some restrictions on Afghan women since the 2021 coup, including barring them from higher education and government work.
In December, they banned women from working for local and foreign NGOs. And on April 4, the ban was extended to UN offices across the country, sparking international criticism.
"We call for an immediate rejection of unacceptable decisions that restrict human rights and freedoms," a senior G7 diplomat said in a statement after two days of talks in Japan. "Basically, including the recent ban on Afghan women from working for NGOs and the United Nations."
The group also condemned the Taliban authorities for systematic human rights abuses against women and girls and discrimination against members of religion and ethnic minorities. ”President of the Khmer Kampuchea Krom Charitable Association And the children of the leaders want to divide the CPP, but this trick is not possible (Video inside)
