Luong Chan is a Khmer Krom family living in Preah Trapeang province. Her family of four, including her husband and two sons, fled to Cambodia during the 1960s. Fleeing to Cambodia was to escape the war between South and North Vietnam. Especially after her husband was wounded in the war.
Luong Chan, who is now over 80 years old, recalled that in order to flee to Cambodia, her husband had to become a priest to deceive the Vietnamese authorities before he could come to Cambodia.
She recalled that after fleeing to Cambodia, she and her husband and children lived and worked for a while in Phnom Penh, then moved to Pursat. Before the arrival of the Pol Pot genocidal regime, the grandmother and her husband and children moved to Sangkom village, a village in Sandan commune, Sambo district, Kratie province.
After several trials, the local authorities released her after about two years in prison.
Breast milk or expensive life After two years of trial and imprisonment, Luong Chan survived the "Vietnamese" accusation. But her husband was not lucky enough to be reunited with his wife and children. Her husband was beaten to death by the Khmer Rouge for allegedly being "Vietnamese". This is a sad and bitter story that will never be forgotten in her life.
According to her, her husband's death was tragic, but at the time, she did not dare to express her grief. If expressed grief, she may also be killed.
The torture of losing her husband is an unforgettable memory for Luong Chan during her time at the Khmer Rouge prison. Besides, hunger and thirst while in prison is also a memory in life. She recalled that because of hunger, even worms and worms had to eat.
The overthrow of the Pol Pot genocidal regime on January 7, 1979, under the resistance movement of the United Front for National Salvation, led by Samdech Heng Samrin, Samdech Chea Sim and Samdech Hun Sen, left Luong Chan and her two sons Live, learn, live like other Cambodian people. She saw that without this liberation, she and her two sons, as well as other people, would not have survived.
By now, the Khmer Rouge regime has been overthrown, the Khmer Rouge leaders have died and been tried. But the pain and suffering of that brutal regime still lingers with Luong Chan, even though she begs for peace of mind and is getting older. What she will never forget and the most painful is the report accusing her and her family of being "Vietnamese".
Like other people, Luong Chan was pleased and happy to see the society grow and develop like this.
