WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Japan, South Korea and the United States have agreed to strengthen security cooperation in response to rising North Korean threats as three countries condemn the ICBM test of an intercontinental ballistic missile. Powered by solid fuel from Pyongyang on Thursday. This was released by the AFP news agency on Saturday, April 15, 2023.
The agreement was reached at the 13th tripartite defense meeting this Friday in Washington, D.C., to exchange assessments on the security environment on the Korean Peninsula and in the region as a whole, as well as consultations on Strong ways to deepen security cooperation between the three countries.
The three countries' joint statement said its officials also discussed regular anti-submarine and air defense drills to counter the North Korean threat.
Analysts say it will be the first time North Korea has used solid fuel in its long-range ballistic missile, or ICBM. The development of ICBMs using solid fuel technology has long been seen as a major goal for North Korea, as it could help Pyongyang deploy and launch its missiles faster in the event of war.
