(Siem Reap): After failing to host Angkor Sankranti for three years due to the Kovid 19 crisis, the number of national and international tourists has dwindled, making their lives very difficult.
In particular, after the Royal Government announced that it will organize Angkor Sankranti in 2023, vendors at Angkor Wat and other temples are very happy, they hope to be able to restore some of the family economy.
Mom, who sells clothes and souvenirs, said she was very happy after having so many customers because she had not had so many guests for so many years due to Kovid's disease. Ms. Ba said that the livelihood of traders in the temples, if there are no national and international tourists, their lives are very difficult because they have no work to do other than raising.
Ms. Prum Samet and Ms. Lem Thin, restaurant vendors in Angkor Wat, said with a smile that she had been selling here for many years, during the time when Kovid 19 had been selling items she had already found. She also volunteered to demolish the house to live in the natural village of Run Ta Ek. These factors made them even more difficult.
She also thanked the policy of the Royal Government, which has Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, who gave poor cards for 10 years to people who volunteered to dismantle their houses and move to Run Ta Ek Natural Village. She added that although the budget is not much, but can help ease their lives to some extent.
At the same time, all vendors at the temple want the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia, especially the Royal Government to organize Angkor Sankranti events in the following years so that they have income to support their families.
