(Phnom Penh): General Prof. Dr. Nop Ratana, Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander of the Artillery Command, emphasized that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, will always remember The merits of ex-soldiers who sacrificed for the motherland.
General Nop Ratana emphasized this during a meeting and visit of retired and disabled veterans who used to work in the Artillery Command this morning, Tuesday, April 11, 2023 at the Artillery Command in Kampong Speu.
During the meeting to visit the retired and disabled veterans on the occasion of the National New Year, General Nop Ratana expressed his deep feelings and deep affection for all the retired and disabled veterans.
At the same time, the General conveyed the greetings of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense, and General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, to the retired and disabled soldiers. All who participated in the social gathering.
At the same time, the General reminded the heroic example of the veterans and disabled soldiers who mourned together on the battlefield for the cause of the motherland. In particular, the fight to defend the territorial integrity from the invading enemies in the area of Preah Vihear, Tamon and Ta Krabei.
General Prof. Dr. Nop Ratana "We will never forget, Excellencies, all of you who have been with us in the bunker. In particular, fighting foreigners who invade us is not uncommon. Fighting in the battle of Preah Vihear in the battle of Ta Moan, Ta Krabey, Excellencies, all of you are the toughest fighters. Your Excellency's fight is the most successful, and Samdech always upholds this merit.
In addition to commemorating the merits of ex-servicemen and disabled soldiers, General Nop Ratana also highlighted that Samdech Techo Hun Sen always pays attention to the well-being of ex-servicemen and advises to organize meetings and greetings for ex-servicemen. For General Nop Ratana, this is a gesture to show Samdech Techo's high concern for ex-soldiers as well as disabled soldiers without forgetting them.
The attention of the head of the Royal Government and the leaders of the unit is to provide strong morale for the troops who have completed their work from the unit.
Sous Thea, 63, has been in the army since 1984. During his military career, Sous Thea fought many battles, including the battles at Preah Vihear, Ta Moan and Ta Krabei. Mr. Sous Thea retired in 2021.
Despite his retirement, Mr. Sous Thea is still happy and warm, receiving high attention and consideration from Samdech Techo Prime Minister and the leadership of the Ministry of National Defense. In particular, General Nop Ratana, Commander of the Artillery Command.
Meas Sophan, 67, is a disabled soldier. He joined the army in 1978 and retired in 2021. Despite leaving the unit, Mr. Meas Sophan still feels warm, receiving consideration from the Prime Minister as well as the leadership of the unit.
